🐢 brains.auth.use — point the global claude cli at a stored subscription

  usage: brains.auth.use [--reach <email>]

    --reach <email>  the account to sign in as
                     alias: --sub (the old name, superseded)
    (no args)        show which account is signed in now

  example: brains.auth.use --reach casey@ahction.com

  whichever account you are on now is detected and stowed automatically —
  even one you signed into by hand — so you can swap back with no browser.
  open claude sessions keep the OLD token in memory — restart them after.

  exit codes with --reach (a SWAP), for a caller that reads only $?:
    0  the swap landed, cleanly
    1  it broke on our side — OR the swap landed but claude's own profile
       view is stale (the tree says which; your credentials are intact)
    2  it needs you — a dead token, a bad flag, or an identity we could not
       verify (a swap is refused rather than risk the live account's token)

  exit codes with no args (a READ) — these differ, and a non-zero here
  means the ANSWER is doubtful, never that a credential moved:
    0  the answer is trustworthy — a verified name, or a confirmed
       absence of any login
    1  a login is present but could not be named — a FAILED read, kept
       apart from 'no login' on purpose
    2  a name was found but not verified — do not act on it unchecked.
       an idle terminal whose access token has expired reads this way,
       so treat it as 'renew, then re-ask', not as an error
